WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.335
Lesson zero point four.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.320
Exchange account versus your own

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.365
wallet: who holds the keys?

00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:08.850
By the end, you'll understand custody, and

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.046
decide what stays on the exchange and

00:00:11.046 --> 00:00:12.780
what moves to your own wallet.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.114
There's a saying in crypto that sounds

00:00:16.114 --> 00:00:18.135
harsh the first time you hear it.

00:00:18.135 --> 00:00:20.170
Not your keys, not your coins.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.235
It means exactly what it says.

00:00:22.235 --> 00:00:23.967
If someone else holds the keys to

00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:26.107
your crypto, you don't control it, you're

00:00:26.107 --> 00:00:27.935
trusting them to give it back.

00:00:27.935 --> 00:00:29.857
This lesson is about understanding

00:00:29.857 --> 00:00:31.560
that trade-off, not fearing it.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.150
Here's the plan.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.888
First, what it means to hold

00:00:35.888 --> 00:00:38.165
crypto on an exchange, custodial.

00:00:38.165 --> 00:00:41.560
Second, what it means to hold it yourself, self-custody.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.215
Third, the full comparison, five ways, side by side.

00:00:45.215 --> 00:00:47.587
And finally, an illustrative scenario,

00:00:47.587 --> 00:00:49.168
your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:50.468 --> 00:00:52.883
Here's the whole lesson in one picture.

00:00:52.883 --> 00:00:54.160
On the left, an exchange

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.788
holding your keys for you.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:56.949
On the right, a wallet

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:58.713
where you hold them yourself.

00:00:58.713 --> 00:01:01.358
Same crypto, two very different arrangements.

00:01:02.658 --> 00:01:03.713
On an exchange.

00:01:05.013 --> 00:01:06.836
Here's how custody works when you

00:01:06.836 --> 00:01:08.637
leave crypto on an exchange.

00:01:08.637 --> 00:01:11.022
The exchange holds the actual keys.

00:01:11.022 --> 00:01:12.127
It owes you the balance

00:01:12.127 --> 00:01:13.532
you see in your account.

00:01:13.532 --> 00:01:15.857
If you forget your password, you can

00:01:15.857 --> 00:01:18.685
recover access with ID, because the exchange

00:01:18.685 --> 00:01:20.808
is the one keeping the books.

00:01:20.808 --> 00:01:23.785
But if the exchange fails, freezes withdrawals, or

00:01:23.785 --> 00:01:26.237
is hacked, your money is at risk, because

00:01:26.237 --> 00:01:28.163
you never held the keys yourself.

00:01:29.463 --> 00:01:30.588
In your own wallet.

00:01:31.888 --> 00:01:34.194
In your own wallet, you hold the keys,

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:36.978
the secret codes that control your crypto.

00:01:36.978 --> 00:01:38.603
Nobody can freeze it.

00:01:38.603 --> 00:01:41.378
But that cuts both ways: nobody can recover it

00:01:41.378 --> 00:01:43.680
for you either, if you lose those keys.

00:01:44.981 --> 00:01:47.531
And here's something worth being precise about.

00:01:47.531 --> 00:01:50.036
A wallet doesn't actually store your coins.

00:01:50.036 --> 00:01:52.851
The coins live on the blockchain, permanently.

00:01:52.851 --> 00:01:54.996
Your seed phrase creates your keys.

00:01:54.996 --> 00:01:56.884
Your keys prove you own an address,

00:01:56.884 --> 00:01:59.176
and let you sign transactions from it.

00:01:59.176 --> 00:02:01.471
The wallet app is just the interface.

00:02:01.471 --> 00:02:04.353
Lose the app, restore the seed phrase into any

00:02:04.353 --> 00:02:07.479
other one, and every key, every address, and every

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:09.748
balance comes back exactly as it was.

00:02:11.048 --> 00:02:14.238
Which brings up two very different pieces of information.

00:02:14.238 --> 00:02:16.703
Your address is like an account number: safe

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:19.358
to share, that's how people send you money.

00:02:19.358 --> 00:02:22.439
Your seed phrase is the master key to everything:

00:02:22.439 --> 00:02:26.198
never shared, never typed into a website, never photographed.

00:02:27.498 --> 00:02:28.588
Quick check.

00:02:28.588 --> 00:02:31.653
Is your crypto actually stored inside your wallet app?

00:02:36.073 --> 00:02:38.608
The answer: no. It's on the blockchain.

00:02:38.608 --> 00:02:40.203
The wallet just holds the keys

00:02:40.203 --> 00:02:41.438
that let you control it.

00:02:42.738 --> 00:02:45.141
Here's exactly why one side can recover

00:02:45.141 --> 00:02:47.603
a lost password and the other can't.

00:02:47.603 --> 00:02:50.779
On an exchange, forgetting your password isn't the

00:02:50.779 --> 00:02:54.017
end: you contact support, verify your identity with

00:02:54.017 --> 00:02:56.943
ID, and they reset access, because they're the

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:59.298
ones keeping the account records.

00:02:59.298 --> 00:03:00.902
In your own wallet, there's

00:03:00.902 --> 00:03:02.748
no support desk to contact.

00:03:02.748 --> 00:03:05.493
Nobody is keeping records on your behalf.

00:03:05.493 --> 00:03:08.605
Only your own seed phrase can rebuild those keys,

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:11.592
which is exactly why losing it, with no backup,

00:03:11.592 --> 00:03:13.708
means the funds are gone for good.

00:03:15.008 --> 00:03:17.857
One more mental model, worth previewing here even

00:03:17.857 --> 00:03:20.493
though Module One goes much deeper on it.

00:03:20.493 --> 00:03:22.823
Custody isn't really all-or-nothing.

00:03:22.823 --> 00:03:25.580
Many people use a small, everyday wallet for

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.581
spending and trying things, and a separate, more

00:03:28.581 --> 00:03:31.889
protected wallet, sometimes on a hardware device, for

00:03:31.889 --> 00:03:34.088
savings they don't touch often.

00:03:34.088 --> 00:03:36.911
Same principle as the exchange-versus-wallet

00:03:36.911 --> 00:03:38.918
split, applied one level deeper.

00:03:40.218 --> 00:03:41.933
Five ways, side by side.

00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:44.828
Who holds the keys?

00:03:44.828 --> 00:03:46.433
The exchange, or you.

00:03:46.433 --> 00:03:49.713
Can you recover access if you forget your password?

00:03:49.713 --> 00:03:52.258
Yes, with ID, on an exchange.

00:03:52.258 --> 00:03:54.963
Only with your seed phrase, in your own wallet.

00:03:54.963 --> 00:03:56.793
Can your funds be frozen?

00:03:56.793 --> 00:03:58.388
Yes, on an exchange.

00:03:58.388 --> 00:04:00.063
No, in your own wallet.

00:04:00.063 --> 00:04:02.093
Can you use DeFi directly?

00:04:02.093 --> 00:04:03.888
Limited, on an exchange.

00:04:03.888 --> 00:04:05.653
Yes, in your own wallet.

00:04:05.653 --> 00:04:07.358
And what's your main risk?

00:04:07.358 --> 00:04:09.523
On an exchange, it's the company.

00:04:09.523 --> 00:04:10.814
In your own wallet, it's

00:04:10.814 --> 00:04:12.260
your own mistakes and scams.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.760
Notice that last row especially.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.368
Self-custody doesn't remove risk, it relocates it, from a

00:04:19.368 --> 00:04:22.405
company you can't control to habits you can.

00:04:22.405 --> 00:04:24.010
That's not a downgrade.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.638
It's a different job, and this whole program is

00:04:26.638 --> 00:04:28.500
here to help you do that job well.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.660
And this isn't just a hypothetical concern.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.088
Crypto's history includes real exchanges that froze

00:04:36.088 --> 00:04:39.319
withdrawals during a crisis, or failed outright,

00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:42.520
sometimes for months, sometimes permanently.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.472
It also includes real people who lost everything

00:04:45.472 --> 00:04:47.641
to a mistake in their own wallet: a

00:04:47.641 --> 00:04:49.990
lost seed phrase, a phishing site.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.595
Both risks are real.

00:04:51.595 --> 00:04:53.855
This program spends real time on both.

00:04:55.155 --> 00:04:56.629
Why does an exchange freeze

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:58.680
withdrawals in the first place?

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.305
A few common reasons.

00:05:00.305 --> 00:05:04.845
A regulatory request, forcing a pause while something's investigated.

00:05:04.845 --> 00:05:08.080
Suspected fraud on an account, theirs or yours.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.168
Or a liquidity crunch, if too many

00:05:10.168 --> 00:05:12.256
people try to withdraw at once and

00:05:12.256 --> 00:05:15.420
the exchange can't meet every request instantly.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.033
None of these can happen to a

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:19.398
wallet only you control, because there's no

00:05:19.398 --> 00:05:21.495
company in the loop to freeze anything.

00:05:22.795 --> 00:05:24.635
And here's why DeFi access is

00:05:24.635 --> 00:05:27.265
“limited” on an exchange specifically.

00:05:27.265 --> 00:05:29.423
An exchange only lets you do what

00:05:29.423 --> 00:05:32.152
its own interface offers: buy, sell, maybe

00:05:32.152 --> 00:05:34.610
a savings product it built itself.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.568
Your own wallet can connect directly to any smart

00:05:37.568 --> 00:05:40.703
contract on the blockchain, which is the entire rest

00:05:40.703 --> 00:05:43.010
of the program, from Module One onward.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.455
So, practically, what goes where?

00:05:46.455 --> 00:05:49.297
Keep on an exchange what you're actively trading,

00:05:49.297 --> 00:05:51.627
or a small amount you check often, since

00:05:51.627 --> 00:05:54.825
you'll want its convenience and its recoverability.

00:05:54.825 --> 00:05:57.256
Move to your own wallet what you're saving

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.404
for the longer term, or what you need

00:05:59.404 --> 00:06:02.457
for DeFi, since that's where control actually matters.

00:06:03.757 --> 00:06:05.282
An illustrative scenario.

00:06:06.582 --> 00:06:08.830
Numbers make trade-offs concrete in a

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.786
way a rule never quite does.

00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:14.335
So before the checklist, here's one hypothetical scenario,

00:06:14.335 --> 00:06:17.283
walked all the way through, showing exactly what

00:06:17.283 --> 00:06:19.990
each side of the comparison table feels like

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.096
when something actually goes wrong.

00:06:23.396 --> 00:06:26.159
Here's a hypothetical, to make this concrete,

00:06:26.159 --> 00:06:28.441
not a real event or a prediction.

00:06:28.441 --> 00:06:31.132
Sam splits $1,000: half stays on an

00:06:31.132 --> 00:06:34.196
exchange for easy trading, half moves to

00:06:34.196 --> 00:06:37.336
Sam's own wallet for DeFi and savings.

00:06:37.336 --> 00:06:39.901
One difficult week, the exchange pauses

00:06:39.901 --> 00:06:43.471
withdrawals while it investigates unusual activity.

00:06:43.471 --> 00:06:46.301
Sam's exchange half is temporarily stuck.

00:06:46.301 --> 00:06:50.161
Sam's wallet half is completely unaffected, and still fully

00:06:50.161 --> 00:06:53.506
usable, because nobody but Sam ever held those keys.

00:06:54.806 --> 00:06:56.971
Notice what this scenario isn't.

00:06:56.971 --> 00:06:59.211
It isn't a claim that exchanges are

00:06:59.211 --> 00:07:02.311
dangerous, or that self-custody is risk-free.

00:07:02.311 --> 00:07:04.605
It's a picture of what “not your keys,

00:07:04.605 --> 00:07:07.193
not your coins” actually looks like when it

00:07:07.193 --> 00:07:09.899
matters, and why many people choose to split,

00:07:09.899 --> 00:07:12.252
rather than put everything in one place.

00:07:13.553 --> 00:07:16.003
One more honest note on Sam's story.

00:07:16.003 --> 00:07:18.611
A split doesn't mean an even fifty-fifty, and

00:07:18.611 --> 00:07:21.293
it isn't a formula this program hands you.

00:07:21.293 --> 00:07:23.928
It's a habit: hold some funds where you'll

00:07:23.928 --> 00:07:27.114
want quick, recoverable access, and move some where

00:07:27.114 --> 00:07:30.055
you want full control, and revisit that balance

00:07:30.055 --> 00:07:31.893
as your own situation changes.

00:07:33.193 --> 00:07:36.342
Here's what actually moving funds from an exchange into

00:07:36.342 --> 00:07:38.948
your own wallet looks like, step by step.

00:07:38.948 --> 00:07:41.148
Copy your wallet's receive address.

00:07:41.148 --> 00:07:43.345
Paste it into the exchange's withdrawal

00:07:43.345 --> 00:07:45.293
field, and check it carefully.

00:07:45.293 --> 00:07:48.722
Confirm the network matches on both sides, sending on

00:07:48.722 --> 00:07:51.753
the wrong one is a real and costly mistake.

00:07:51.753 --> 00:07:53.878
Send a small test amount first.

00:07:53.878 --> 00:07:55.368
Confirm it arrives.

00:07:55.368 --> 00:07:56.883
And only then send the rest.

00:07:58.183 --> 00:07:59.363
Checklist and quiz.

00:08:00.663 --> 00:08:02.558
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:08:02.558 --> 00:08:06.983
You can explain custodial versus self-custody in plain words.

00:08:06.983 --> 00:08:08.704
You know your seed phrase is the

00:08:08.704 --> 00:08:10.673
wallet, not just a backup of it.

00:08:10.673 --> 00:08:12.622
And you know an address is safe to

00:08:12.622 --> 00:08:14.961
share, while a seed phrase never, ever is.

00:08:16.261 --> 00:08:17.506
Question two.

00:08:17.506 --> 00:08:18.934
What does “not your keys,

00:08:18.934 --> 00:08:20.581
not your coins” actually mean?

00:08:25.001 --> 00:08:27.606
The answer: if someone else holds the keys, you

00:08:27.606 --> 00:08:30.076
depend on them to give your crypto back.

00:08:30.076 --> 00:08:33.146
It's not automatically yours to move whenever you want.

00:08:34.446 --> 00:08:35.716
Question three.

00:08:35.716 --> 00:08:37.539
Which can you safely share: your

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.141
address, or your seed phrase?

00:08:43.561 --> 00:08:45.306
The answer: your address.

00:08:45.306 --> 00:08:47.726
Never your seed phrase, to anyone, ever.

00:08:49.026 --> 00:08:50.281
Question four.

00:08:50.281 --> 00:08:53.616
Your funds are frozen by an exchange investigation.

00:08:53.616 --> 00:08:55.641
Is that possible in your own wallet too?

00:09:00.061 --> 00:09:02.307
The answer: no. Nobody but you holds

00:09:02.307 --> 00:09:04.250
the keys in your own wallet, so

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.311
nobody but you can freeze it.

00:09:06.311 --> 00:09:08.186
The trade-off is that nobody but

00:09:08.186 --> 00:09:09.721
you can recover it, either.

00:09:11.021 --> 00:09:13.831
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:13.831 --> 00:09:16.038
On an exchange, they hold the keys,

00:09:16.038 --> 00:09:19.036
easy, but recoverable and freezable by them.

00:09:19.036 --> 00:09:21.439
In your own wallet, you hold the keys,

00:09:21.439 --> 00:09:24.696
immune to freezing, but recoverable only by you.

00:09:24.696 --> 00:09:28.234
Compare the two, honestly, across all five dimensions.

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:29.938
And decide what stays where,

00:09:29.938 --> 00:09:31.584
on purpose, not by accident.

00:09:32.883 --> 00:09:35.928
This is education, not financial advice.

00:09:35.928 --> 00:09:37.275
Where you hold your crypto

00:09:37.275 --> 00:09:38.823
is your decision to make.

00:09:38.823 --> 00:09:41.578
Base it on your own comfort with each trade-off.

00:09:41.578 --> 00:09:44.482
And nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:09:44.482 --> 00:09:47.509
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:09:48.809 --> 00:09:51.054
That's exchange versus your own wallet.

00:09:51.054 --> 00:09:52.762
Neither is simply better, they're

00:09:52.762 --> 00:09:55.173
different jobs, with different trade-offs.

00:09:55.173 --> 00:09:57.591
Next up, Lesson zero point five: setting

00:09:57.591 --> 00:09:59.538
up your wallet and backing it up.
